===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*marhaz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*márkos||horse}}. Cognate with {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{m|ofs|mar||horse}}, {{m|ofs|merie||mare}} ({{etyl|fy|-}} {{m|fy|merje||mare}}), {{etyl|osx|-}} {{m|osx|merge}}, {{m|osx|meriha||mare}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|merrie||mare}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|marah||horse}}, {{m|goh|meriha||mare}} ({{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|Mähre||mare}}), {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|marr||horse}} ({{etyl|is|-}} {{m|is|mar}}), {{m|non|merr||mare}} ({{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|märr||mare}}), {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌷𐍃}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of {{etyl|ga|-}} {{m|ga|marc}} and {{etyl|cy|-}} {{m|cy|march||horse}}.

